; `if` has compared its two branches since the first checker. `match`
; and `cond` never compared theirs: the accumulator was overwritten
; with each arm's type in turn, so the form took the LAST arm's type
; and every earlier arm went unchecked at any type at all.
;
; That is a wrong answer, not a missing warning. Measured, with a
; compiler from before 2026-08-17:
;
;   (:: name (-> Color String))
;   (fn (name c) (match c ((Red) 1) ((Green) 2) ((Blue) "blue")))
;   (strLen (name (Red)))     ->  `check` OK, exit 139, no output
;
; The `Int` from the first arm reached `strLen` as a Str header
; address. Because the declared return type is compared against the
; LAST arm only, neither check caught the other's blind spot: the
; signature agreed with `"blue"` and nothing ever looked at `1`.
;
; The relation is `tyCompat`, the same one `if` spends - not the
; narrow `tyReprClash` the SIGNATURE-vs-body comparison needs. Two
; branches of one form are both INFERRED, so neither is a declaration
; anyone is punning a handle through, which is why `if` could afford
; the strict relation from the beginning and why these two can now.
; Measured: the strict relation over `match` and `cond` reports 0 of
; the 261 files the clean-corpus sweep covers.

(data Color (Red) (Green) (Blue))

; The arms disagree; the report anchors at the arm that broke ranks.
(:: nameOf (-> Color String))
(fn (nameOf c)
  (match c
    ((Red) 1)
    ((Green) "green")
    ((Blue) "blue")))

; The same hole one door down, in `cond`.
(:: sizeOf (-> Int String))
(fn (sizeOf n)
  (cond
    ((> n 100) "big")
    ((> n 2) 7)
    (else "small")))

(:: main Int)
(fn (main) 0)
